      "blocks": {
        "q1": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Which panel height do I need for a pool fence in Texas?",
            "answer": "<p>Most cities in Texas apply the 48\" pool barrier rule from the IRC and ISPSC. Texas Health and Safety Code chapter 757 sets 48\" for apartment-complex and HOA pools. The 46\" panel in a 6\" spigot makes a 48\" railing, the usual pool fence height here. If you want more height, the 57.7\" panel makes a 60\" fence. Confirm barrier and gate rules with your city before ordering.</p>",
            "pages": ""
          }
        },
        "q2": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Do Texas sun and heat affect the glass or the hardware?",
            "answer": "<p>The materials handle Texas sun. The panels are 12 mm (1/2\") tempered safety glass with a lifetime warranty against manufacturing defects. Every spigot, connector and hinge is 2205 marine-grade stainless steel with a 10-year no-rust promise. That promise applies in humid Houston and in dry West Texas alike. A practical note: glass in full afternoon sun gets hot to the touch in July, like any metal or glass outdoors.</p>",
            "pages": ""
          }
        },
        "q3": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Do I need a permit for a glass railing in Texas?",
            "answer": "<p>It depends on your city and the project. Texas has no statewide residential code for cities, so each city runs its own permit process with local amendments. A new deck, balcony or pool barrier typically needs a permit and an inspection. Replacing a railing on an existing deck is sometimes handled differently. Ask your building department before ordering; our engineering documents cover the spigot attachments if they ask.</p>",
            "pages": ""
          }
        },
        "q4": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Will the hardware hold up to salt air in Galveston or Corpus Christi?",
            "answer": "<p>Every spigot, connector, hinge and latch is 2205 marine-grade stainless steel with a 10-year no-rust promise, and that promise applies on the coast. An occasional fresh-water rinse removes salt film, as on any boat hardware. Coastal cities have their own wind rules for exterior work, so ask your building department. Our engineering documents cover the spigot attachments on wood and concrete.</p>",
            "pages": ""
          }
        }
      },
