        "t1": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p><strong>Where Texans install glass railings</strong></p><p>Texas is really four or five markets, and the typical project changes from one to the next. In <a href=\"/pages/texas-glass-railing-dallas\">Dallas</a>, Fort Worth, Plano and Frisco, the usual jobs are glass pool fences and second-floor balcony railings on two-story homes. In <a href=\"/pages/texas-glass-railing-houston\">Houston</a>, The Woodlands, Katy and Sugar Land, think pool decks, covered patios and rooftop decks on inner-loop townhomes. <a href=\"/pages/texas-glass-railing-austin\">Austin</a> and the Hill Country are deck country: view decks over limestone slopes and waterfront decks on Lake Travis. In <a href=\"/pages/texas-glass-railing-san-antonio\">San Antonio</a>, the Alamo Heights, Stone Oak and Dominion areas have the outdoor living spaces and view patios that suit frameless glass. On the coast, in Corpus Christi, Galveston and Port Aransas, salt air and wind make the 2205 marine-grade stainless hardware the sensible choice. Far West Texas and the Panhandle get the same crate, the same hardware and the same freight delivery.</p>"
          }
        },
        "t2": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p><strong>Texas building codes for guards and pool barriers</strong></p><p>Texas has no single statewide residential code for cities. Each city adopts its own edition of the International Residential Code and International Building Code. Houston, Dallas, Austin and San Antonio are on the 2021 editions with local amendments. The code sets a 36\" minimum guard height for houses and 42\" for commercial and multifamily buildings. For pool barriers, most cities in Texas apply the 48\" rule from the IRC and ISPSC. Texas Health and Safety Code chapter 757 sets the same 48\" for apartment-complex and HOA pools. Our 33.6\" panel makes a 36\" railing, the 39.6\" panel a 42\" railing and the 46\" panel a 48\" railing. Smaller towns and counties can differ, so check with your local building department before you order. The full summary is on our <a href=\"/pages/texas-building-code-for-glass-railings\">Texas building code page</a>.</p>"
          }
        },
        "t3": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p><strong>Delivery to Texas from our warehouse</strong></p><p>Every order ships from our warehouse in Sainte-Julie, Quebec, and the process is the same whether you are in El Paso or Beaumont. Orders are processed in 2 to 3 business days while the glass is crated. Delivery by freight carrier then takes 5 to 10 business days anywhere in the continental U.S. The truck has a tailgate lift, so the crate comes down to street level at your driveway or job site. Custom glass takes 2 to 3 business days of processing and then 2 to 3 weeks of shipping. Shipping is free on orders over 3,000 dollars. The price you see at checkout is final: no duties, tariffs or hidden fees, even though the crate crosses the border from Canada. Check the glass within 3 days of delivery and before installation. If anything arrived damaged, we replace or refund it at no cost.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
